c05-52f7-9c69-e44c85afd9d1'}, {'abstract': 'Beck, Kendrick Lamar and Tom Waits articulate the creative impulse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/01/t-magazine/beck-tom-waits-kendrick-lamar.html', 'snippet': 'Beck, Kendrick Lamar and Tom Waits articulate the creative impulse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LATE LAST YEAR, a week before his final record was released and not quite four weeks before he died, Leonard Cohen held a press conference at the Canadian consulate in Los Angeles. Dapper in a dark suit, seeking support in his seat from a cane, the 82-year-old fielded questions. With a wit and candor and gentleness and wisdom that felt extraterrestrial, he spoke as though he’d traveled here from a civilization more advanced than our own to offer us a few gifts that could ensure the survival of our species. Near the end, there was a kind of apotheosis. A Japanese reporter asked Cohen about one of the lyrics from the new record’s title song, “You Want It Darker,” in which Cohen sing-speaks, “Hineni, hineni, I’m ready my Lord.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '96', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/01/t-magazine/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy/musicians-slide-ZS3E-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1038, 'width': 1556, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Three Iconic Musicians on Artistic Creation — and Its Importance Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fires They Got', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lamar, Kendrick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-01T17:01:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wyatt Mason', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wyatt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mason', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/919d3ed6-ff40-58f2-bfab-a2363e02f1f6', 'word_count': 4193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/919d3ed6-ff40-58f2-bfab-a2363e02f1f6'}, {'abstract': 'When a wayward bull led the police on a wild chase in Queens last week, what they really needed was a cowboy with a lasso.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/01/nyregion/when-a-bull-is-loose-in-the-city-why-not-call-on-urban-cowboys.html', 'snippet': 'When a wayward bull led the police on a wild chase in Queens last week, what they really needed was a cowboy with a lasso.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For two hours last week a bull ran through the streets of Jamaica, Queens, trotting between parked cars and across lawns after escaping from a slaughterhouse. All the while, the police engaged in a frenetic and televised pursuit, as they tried to corner the animal with a police car (it jumped over the hood) and bring it down with tranquilizer darts (for a time, the bull kept going despite the sedative.)', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 200, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 200}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 341, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/nyregion/02COWBOY3/02COWBOY3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When a Bull is Loose in the City, Why Not Call on Urban Cowboys?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When a Bull Runs Loose, Why Not Call a Cowboy?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cattle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cowboys and Cowgirls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-01T17:02:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff1e0d18-ae66-5015-93ef-537ace619c2b', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff1e0d18-ae66-5015-93ef-537ace619c2b'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffrey Deitch has decorated a booth at the Armory Show in the style of Florine Stettheimer’s original space — with contributions from contemporary painters like Cecily Brown and John Currin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/florine-stettheimer-studio-jeffrey-deitch.html', 'snippet': 'Jeffrey Deitch has decorated a booth at the Armory Show in the style of Florine Stettheimer’s original space — with contributions from contemporary painters like Cecily Brown and John Currin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1995, at the Gramercy International Art Fair, the art dealer Jeffrey Deitch, who at the time had yet to open his own gallery, created a kind of reproduction of Florine Stettheimer’s studio. Stettheimer was a pioneering modernist and feminist painter who died in 1944 and for many years was little known outside of a small circle of art historians and admirers. Deitch filled the Gramercy Hotel’s penthouse with ornate furniture and cellophane curtains to approximate Stettheimer’s studio at 80 West 40th Street, where she hosted one of the city’s great salons, which counted among its guests the critic Henry McBride, as well as Georgia O’Keefe and Marcel Duchamp. (Stettheimer hosted Duchamp’s 30th birthday, a scene that she later painted.) In 1996, Deitch would open his gallery — Deitch Projects, in SoHo, known for its performative exhibitions and decadent events — using Stettheimer as a kind of model.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/art/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E/01tmag-deitch-slide-YN2E-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1384, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pioneering Feminist Artist’s Studio Gets Revived in New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMORY SHOW, THE INTERNATIONAL FAIR OF NEW ART (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deitch Projects', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stettheimer, Florine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-01T17:10:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Art', 'byline': {'original': 'By M.H. Miller', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8091133-1874-58c7-ac46-cc3661db982d', 'word_count': 643, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8091133-1874-58c7-ac46-cc3661db982d'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese manufacturers once reaped big benefits from a falling renminbi, but heavy debt and intense competition now reduce the positive impact.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/01/business/china-dollar-yuan-renminbi.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese manufacturers once reaped big benefits from a falling renminbi, but heavy debt and intense competition now reduce the positive impact.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOSHAN, China — At first glance, given the way that China controls i