al artists, used to work regularly as an art critic — and basically still does, in a continuing series of brilliant text drawings produced over the past decade. Many of them, done in graphite and colored pencil, are trompe l’oeil images: sheets of crumpled notebook paper covered with handwritten lists and statements excoriating the art world as a gatekeeping money machine. The work is Unabomber-crazy, very funny and depressingly on target.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE2/05GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'William Powhida, Political and Not Amused', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; He’s Political, and Not Amused', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aldrich Contemporary Art Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Powhida, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-01T17:32:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc1e3103-90ab-56ad-9608-44c49bce82b0', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc1e3103-90ab-56ad-9608-44c49bce82b0'}, {'abstract': 'The three-night festival celebrates the six-string, with an emphasis on the communal and exploratory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/01/arts/music/alternative-guitar-summit-dont-expect-three-chords-and-the-truth.html', 'snippet': 'The three-night festival celebrates the six-string, with an emphasis on the communal and exploratory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the blues to the experimental fringe, the guitar usually represents some promise of self-possession, assertion, independence. The annual Alternative Guitar Summit is something rare: a celebration of the guitar in a communal, sum-of-its-parts context. And this year its founder, the guitarist Joel Harrison, is focusing more than ever on ensembles with multiple six-stringers.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/arts/05GUIDE7/05GUIDE7-thumbStanda