cker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Suzhou (China)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridges and Tunnels', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T09:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Owen Guo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Owen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Guo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54a41e7f-b0df-5b79-8546-c050bc62fdc4', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54a41e7f-b0df-5b79-8546-c050bc62fdc4'}, {'abstract': '“Someone looking at the United States would have to be worried about democratic failure.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/opinion/how-the-internet-threatens-democracy.html', 'snippet': '“Someone looking at the United States would have to be worried about democratic failure.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the forces of reaction outpace movements predicated on the ideal of progress, and as traditional norms of political competition are tossed aside, it’s clear that the internet and social media have succeeded in doing what many feared and some hoped they would. They have disrupted and destroyed institutional constraints on what can be said, when and where it can be said and who can say it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/02/opinion/02edsallWeb/02edsallWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1334, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Democracy, Disrupted', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T09:20:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas B. Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b97b6fa1-6a74-5170-a323-87253bbf90d4', 'word_count': 1891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b97b6fa1-6a74-5170-a323-87253bbf90d4'}, {'abstract': 'As is the case with so many of nature’s features, some scientists think climate change and resource depletion will make more rivers more ephemeral.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-arroyos.html', 'snippet': 'As is the case with so many of nature’s features, some scientists think climate change and resource depletion will make more rivers more ephemeral.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this year, while driving through one of those lulling stretches of desert sameness, on the way to visit family in Arizona, I came to a river. Or better to say, I came to a sign that warned me of an approaching river. A ribbon of trees, which stood out cloudy green against the fractal red desert, seemed to corroborate its story. Up here, the scene said, water. Except that, as I passed over the river’s otherwise imposing bridge, the view turned grim. In both directions ran a broad channel of sand and tumbleweeds, clotted with grass and littered with logs bent into X formations, like Czech hedgehogs at a military checkpoint. Dry, inert and ugly, it didn’t seem to be a river at all.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: Arroyos', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Arroyos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noah Gallagher Shannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': 'Gallagher', 'lastname': 'Shannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e233cde-e921-555d-83bd-e4d266454be4', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e233cde-e921-555d-83bd-e4d266454be4'}, {'abstract': 'She managed her autoimmune arthritis by taking medications. But then something strange started happening to her hands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/magazine/why-was-her-thumb-turning-black.html', 'snippet': 'She managed her autoimmune arthritis by taking medications. But then something strange started happening to her hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 72-year-old woman carefully loosened the bandage she had wrapped around her thumb. As she gently revealed the injured finger, her daughter gasped. The top third of what should have been the fleshy part looked eaten away. The flesh that remained was black and hard. A foul odor emanated from the wound. “Yesterday I thought there was a dead mouse in my living room,” the woman told her daughter. “Then I realized it was my thumb.” She couldn’t do anything with that hand now, not even work in her garden. It was very upsetting.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05diagnosis1/05diagnosis1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05diagnosis1/05diagnosis1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05diagnosis1/05diagnosis1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05diagnosis1/05diagnosis1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 556, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/05/magazine/05diagnosis1/05diag