 'Jackman, Hugh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Patrick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Logan (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T14:22:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By MEKADO MURPHY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MURPHY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/3cc375ba-3988-5885-8c7d-fd47eefb9748', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/3cc375ba-3988-5885-8c7d-fd47eefb9748'}, {'abstract': 'The director discusses a sequence with Hugh Jackman and Patrick Stewart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/movies/james-mangold-interview-logan.html', 'snippet': 'The director discusses a sequence with Hugh Jackman and Patrick Stewart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While there’s plenty of action in the new Wolverine movie “Logan,” this scene, where Wolverine (played by Hugh Jackman) is giving Professor X (Patrick Stewart) his medication, takes the X-Men franchise to a more fragile place. The director James Mangold wanted to make a superhero movie that felt more like a family drama, and during a recent interview he explained how and why he made that happen with “Logan.” Here are excerpts from that conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'James Mangold Narrates a Scene From ‘Logan’', 'kicker': 'Anatomy of a Scene', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mangold, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackman, Hugh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Patrick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Logan (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T14:27:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d57a3996-8ad4-5fe7-9a88-ec85a6bdbd09', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d57a3996-8ad4-5fe7-9a88-ec85a6bdbd09'}, {'abstract': 'From Vincenzo De Cotiis‘s limited-edition collection of sculptural pieces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/t-magazine/design/vincenzo-de-cotiis-progetto-domestico-cabinet.html', 'snippet': 'From Vincenzo De Cotiis‘s limited-edition collection of sculptural pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Italian architect Vincenzo De Cotiis, known for blending the raw and the refined, has no interest in leveraging fame for a mass-produced furniture line. Instead, in between commissions, he retreats to his Milan atelier to add to Progetto Domestico, his limited-edition collection of sculptural pieces. This six-foot-high cabinet is fashioned from hand-soldered planes of silver-plated brass remarkably made to resemble a mirror. It hangs like a massive armored shell, opening to reveal four rows of crystalline glass shelves.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '82', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/t-magazine/05tmag-02qual_thing-t_CA0/05tmag-02qual_thing-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/05/t-magazine/05tmag-02qual_thing-t_CA0/05tmag-02qual_thing-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumb