known for her portraits — of dead people. For the first time, she shares the bright, colorful London space where she lives and works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maggi Hambling is an artist who has long been preoccupied by death — but her South London studio couldn’t feel more alive. The lobby, for starters, is painted an exuberant shade of ocher — and filled with small clusters of paintings, sketches and a large Rococo-style papier-mâché mirror. A wooden chair, carved in the shape of an elephant, guards the open-plan kitchen and living quarters — which are also home to toy parrots, Mexican masks, cowhide rugs and a contorted Euphorbia plant called Esmeralda. The two-story duplex is spirited, engaging and fiercely unusual — much like its owner.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/01/t-magazine/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q/01tmag-hambling-slide-7O7Q-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, '