.html', 'snippet': 'In this comedy directed by Kris Avedisian, frenemies from high school rediscover each other for better and worse when one returns to their hometown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Observing the reunion of two former high school friends in Kris Avedisian’s squirm-inducing comedy “Donald Cried” is like watching an ostensibly friendly game of catch in which the players tossing a baseball throw it so hard that it becomes a potentially lethal weapon. At first you root for one side to knock out the other, but the balance of sympathy slowly shifts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1263, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03DONALD/03DONALD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Reunited, and It Feels So Awkward in ‘Donald Cried’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reunited,  and It Feels  So Awkward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Donald Cried (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Avedisian, Kris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wakeman, Jesse', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T22:36:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cf3f1e0-486c-5583-9bc0-37926265944a', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cf3f1e0-486c-5583-9bc0-37926265944a'}, {'abstract': 'In this film from Singapore, the chief executioner at a high-security prison takes on a new assistant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/movies/apprentice-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this film from Singapore, the chief executioner at a high-security prison takes on a new assistant.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The trick is to place the knot just behind the left ear and above the jaw.” Those instructions on how to carry out a “humane” hanging are given early in the Singaporean film “Apprentice” by Rahim (Wan Hanafi Su), the chief executioner at a high-security prison, to his new assistant, Aiman (Fir Rahman). Rahim takes enormous pride in his work, which he has been doing for 30 years, and he, at least outwardly, exhibits no qualms about his profession. When their time comes, he boasts, the death-row prisoners feel no pain because they die instantly. Rahim assures one man before he’s hanged that he is being sent to “a better place.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1254, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/03/arts/03APPRENTICE/03APPRENTICE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Apprentice,’ Teaching the Fine Art of Execution', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Playing Hangman, in a Penitentiary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Apprentice (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Singapore', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boo Junfeng', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-02T22:38:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed219bb5-b0cd-56c3-901d-104169529629', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed219bb5-b0cd-56c3-901d-104169529629'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/books/review/10-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, we’ve got a number of books on our list that are certain to get people talking. Cordelia Fine’s latest, “Testosterone Rex,” addresses head on some of the sexism that poses as neuroscience. Barry Friedman’s important new book, “Unwarranted,” takes on the volatile question of how to address abusive policing. And Angie Thomas’s “The Hate U Give” offers teenage readers a way to look at the Black Lives Matter movement. For sheer fun and entertainment? I recommend Bill Schutt’s “Cannibalism.” As readers will discover, that’s not as strange as it sounds. Bon appetit.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/books/review/05edchoice-covers/05edchoice-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 290, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legac