nt has long denounced articles by Western news organizations as biased and dishonest, but Beijing has now adopted a very Western term to describe them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — “FAKE NEWS,” a Twitter post declared. “Prejudice-based,” another said. “Cleverly orchestrated lies,” a news article asserted.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Response to Reports of Torture: ‘Fake News’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China’s Response to Reports of Man’s Torture: ‘Fake News’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torture', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "People's Daily", 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Xinhua', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xie Yang', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-03T13:28:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/242bfea7-d02a-5fdb-9ed0-077e3faa2730', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/242bfea7-d02a-5fdb-9ed0-077e3faa2730'}, {'abstract': 'The pastry chef tours new neighborhoods in his bakery’s minivan and finds time to hone his Taiwanese cooking skills.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/03/nyregion/dominique-ansel-cronut-sunday-routine.html', 'snippet': 'The pastry chef tours new neighborhoods in his bakery’s minivan and finds time to hone his Taiwanese cooking skills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Years before his Cronut inspired New Yorkers to line up around the block in SoHo, Dominique Ansel captured the attention of the French military. At 19, conscription sent the budding chef to French Guiana, where he provided culinary instruction and tore through ravines with a machete. “Like Indiana Jones,” he said. After stints at Fauchon in Paris and Daniel in New York, he invented the Cronut and started a global pastry empire, including bakeries in London and Tokyo and, later this year, his first full-fledged restaurant in Los Angeles. Mr. Ansel, 39, lives with his girlfriend and business partner, Amy Ma, in Columbus Circle.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/nyregion/05ROUTINE1/05ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/05/nyregion/05ROUTINE1/05ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/nyregion/05ROUTINE1/05ROUTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/nyregion/05ROUTINE