 None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bbefbc0-197a-51f6-8f09-7d921eb7ce8a', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bbefbc0-197a-51f6-8f09-7d921eb7ce8a'}, {'abstract': 'In 1967, South Vietnam had a chance at democratic stability. Instead, two rival generals tore it apart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/03/opinion/the-feud-that-sank-saigon.html', 'snippet': 'In 1967, South Vietnam had a chance at democratic stability. Instead, two rival generals tore it apart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Buddhists against Catholics. Northerners against southerners. Civilians against the military. Capital against periphery. Ethnic Vietnamese against ethnic minorities. In 1967, anti-Communist South Vietnam was a caldron of overlapping rivalries, precipitating and reinforcing the political chaos consuming the country after President Ngo Dinh Diem’s 1963 assassination during a military coup.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/04/opinion/04fear2web/04fear2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/04/opinion/04fear2web/04fear2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/04/opinion/04fear2web/04fear2web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/04/opinion/04fear2web/04fear2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/04/opinion/04fear2web/04fear2web-articleLarge.jpg