-03-04T21:19:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Finer and Robert Malley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/04efc8c3-cc80-5f8e-b3f0-fc9e0ae9de1d', 'word_count': 1379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04efc8c3-cc80-5f8e-b3f0-fc9e0ae9de1d'}, {'abstract': 'Vladimir Putin projects Russian might, but a decaying provincial town tells a different story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/04/opinion/sunday/this-is-the-russia-youre-so-afraid-of.html', 'snippet': 'Vladimir Putin projects Russian might, but a decaying provincial town tells a different story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DONSKOY, Russia — The year before he retired from the Soviet equivalent of a mayoral post in a small Russian town, my grandfather went to Turkmenistan to visit his old friend Redzhep, a comrade from his Red Army artillery unit during World War II. Redzhep, like my grandfather, had made good of his postwar career, becoming an agronomist. As my grandfather packed for his rail journey back to Russia, Redzhep came to him in a fit of Central Asian generosity: Ivan, he said, I will give you a freight car of melons to take home.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/05/opinion/sunday/05ANTONOVA/05ANTONOVA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/05/opinion/sunday/05ANTONOVA/05ANTONOVA-articleLarge.jp