': '2017-03-06T05:12:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8827bde-fe9e-5cc2-89fb-5a0fa970d2cc', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8827bde-fe9e-5cc2-89fb-5a0fa970d2cc'}, {'abstract': 'A group of freewheeling young talents is crafting objects from trash, lumpy versions of the female form and deli meat-inspired fabric loafs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/06/t-magazine/young-designers-katie-stout-misha-kahn-chris-wolston.html', 'snippet': 'A group of freewheeling young talents is crafting objects from trash, lumpy versions of the female form and deli meat-inspired fabric loafs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 28-year-old artist and designer Katie Stout, based in Brooklyn’s Bedford-Stuyvesant, has dubbed her oeuvre “naïve pop,” which is apt, considering the inspirations for her chipper, animated furnishings: Dolly Parton, Raymour & Flanigan, Charlotte Perriand, bagels. With rugs that look like giant floppy hats thrown on the floor, intentionally droopy knit chairs and her Girl lamps, which evoke the female form in a way she terms “lumpy and imperfect,” her goal is to make subversive objects that are “feel-good” but “have self-awareness.” Many aren’t entirely functional, like the knit chairs, which are too soft to sit on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/06/t-magazine/06tmag-designers-slide-GUNU/06tmag-designers-slide-GUNU-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/06/t-magazine/06tmag-designers-slide-GUNU/06tmag-designers-slide-GUNU-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',