e', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1bd0d5f-0647-5d94-9779-5367547db8c8', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1bd0d5f-0647-5d94-9779-5367547db8c8'}, {'abstract': 'If democratic leaders had seized the chance to get Russia out of World War I, Lenin, Stalin, even Hitler would have been footnotes in history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/06/opinion/the-february-revolution-and-kerenskys-missed-opportunity.html', 'snippet': 'If democratic leaders had seized the chance to get Russia out of World War I, Lenin, Stalin, even Hitler would have been footnotes in history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brisbane, Australia — The February Revolution is one of history’s great “What if” moments. If this revolution — which actually took place in early March 1917 according to the West’s Gregorian calendar (Russia adopted that calendar only later) — had succeeded in producing a constitutional democracy in place of the czarist empire as its leaders hoped, the world would be a very different place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/08quiggin-inyt/06quiggin-INYT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 445, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/08quiggin-inyt/06quiggin-INYT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 445}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/08quiggin-inyt/06quiggin-INYT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':