 devoted to the Japanese writer Misato Mochizuki revealed music of sometimes startling sensuality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/06/arts/music/review-misato-mochizuki-a-composer-inspired-by-quarks-and-chromosomes-miller-theater.html', 'snippet': 'What was billed as the first substantial American concert devoted to the Japanese writer Misato Mochizuki revealed music of sometimes startling sensuality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a composer announces that her work is inspired by genetics, atomic particles, Möbius strips and the theoretical writings of Roland Barthes, you’d be forgiven for expecting thorny music. Perhaps it makes sense, then, that what was billed as the first substantial concert in America devoted to Misato Mochizuki, a Japanese composer who is based in Paris and draws on a wide and cerebral range of influences, took place at Columbia University.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: A Composer Inspired by Quarks and Chromosomes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Composer Inspired by Quarks and Chromosomes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mochizuki, Misato (1969- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miller Theater at Columbia University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-06T22:23:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b61c83c-aa37-5f2c-a344-2c22cc61b823', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b61c83c-aa37-5f2c-a34