iginal': 'By Mark Atwood Lawrence', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': 'Atwood', 'lastname': 'Lawrence', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/356c32a4-554b-5c88-8a48-4feb81e608fe', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/356c32a4-554b-5c88-8a48-4feb81e608fe'}, {'abstract': 'If the Russian model of love is reckless, the American one involves too much calculation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/07/opinion/what-romantic-regime-are-you-in.html', 'snippet': 'If the Russian model of love is reckless, the American one involves too much calculation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Polina Aronson spent her first 16 years in Russia. There, people tend to regard love as a sort of divine madness that descends from the heavens. Love is regarded, as the sociologist Julia Lerner put it, as “a destiny, a moral act and a value; it is irresistible, it requires sacrifice and implies suffering and pain.” Russians measure one another by how well they are able to bear the upheaval love brings, sometimes to an absurd degree.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/07brooksWeb/07brooksWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1353, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Romantic Regime Are You In?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Regime for Love Are You In?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seventeen (Magazine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-07T08:21:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/48c76996-8918-5b64-9693-e842699c944f', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48c76996-8918-5b64-9693-e842699c944f'}, {'abstract': 'All ideas need testing in open debate, or they become mere prejudices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/07/opinion/smothering-speech-at-middlebury.html', 'snippet': 'All ideas need testing in open debate, or they become mere prejudices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How to begin an editorial about a violent free-speech debacle at Middlebury College in Vermont? Maybe with some words from John Stuart Mill about the importance of giving despised dissenters a chance to speak. “Truth would lose something by their silence,” Mill wrote, even if their views go against the entire world, and the entire world is right.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1460, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/07/opinion/07tue3web/07tue3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smothering Speech at Middlebury', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smothering Speech at Middlebury', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Middlebury College', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Charles Alan (1943- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vermont', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-07T08:21:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/68788eac-ba8d-5446-93d3-546bd08764d9', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68788eac-ba8d-5446-93d3-546bd08764d9'}, {'abstract': 'As Reagan learned, the public will not tolerate threats to their health or the environment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/07/opinion/a-lesson-trump-and-the-epa-should-heed.html', 'snippet': 'As Reagan learned, the public will not tolerate threats to their health or the environment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March 1983, President Ronald Reagan asked me to return to Washington to run the Environmental Protection Agency. I had been the E.P.A.’s first administrator, from 1970 to 1973, and over the agency’s first 10 years, it made enormous progress in bringing the country’s worst pollution problems under control despite resistance from polluting industries and their lobbyists. A worried and outraged public had demanded action, and the government responded.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': Non