alifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/60c70fa5-aef6-575c-8729-c133e01626a6', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60c70fa5-aef6-575c-8729-c133e01626a6'}, {'abstract': 'The Turkish president was outraged by Germany’s barring two of his ministers yet he has worked hard to silence a free press at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/08/opinion/mr-erdogans-jaw-dropping-hypocrisy.html', 'snippet': 'The Turkish president was outraged by Germany’s barring two of his ministers yet he has worked hard to silence a free press at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Turkey’s president, Recep Tayyip Erdogan, has gall. He has jailed tens of thousands of people, shuttered more than 150 media companies and called a referendum in April to enlarge his powers. Yet when local authorities in Germany, for security reasons, barred two Turkish ministers from campaigning on his behalf among Turks living in Germany, Mr. Erdogan exploded, accusing Germany of Nazi practices and knowing nothing about democracy. If he himself was barred from speaking in the country, he warned, he’d “set the world on fire.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/08wed3web/08wed3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/08wed3web/08wed3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/08/opinion/08wed3web/08wed3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'