 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'This Beautiful Fantastic (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Findlay, Jessica Brown', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilkinson, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irvine, Jeremy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aboud, Simon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-09T21:57:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2123b17e-5e96-58cb-ba12-107c03933137', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2123b17e-5e96-58cb-ba12-107c03933137'}, {'abstract': 'A documentary follows bottle and can collectors in New York as they toil to fill their carts for $30 or $40 a day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/09/movies/canners-review.html', 'snippet': 'A documentary follows bottle and can collectors in New York as they toil to fill their carts for $30 or $40 a day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Manfred Kirchheimer, born in Germany in 1931, is an indispensable New York filmmaker, a noticer and a listener without peer. His best-known work is probably “Stations of the Elevated,” a short documentary from 1981 that is a wordless, jazzy, poetic hymn to public transportation, graffiti and the battered glory of a rough time in the city’s history. Though his new feature, “Canners,” takes place in the present, a relatively safer and more prosperous era, it demonstrates that New York remains, in several senses, a tough place.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1146, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10CANNERS/10CANNERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Canners,’ One Man’s Trash Is Another’s Nickel Treasure', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Man’s Trash,  Another’s Nickel Treasure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Canners (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirchheimer, Manfred', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-09T21:58:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/22082625-2a06-598b-b062-9429b3437573', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22082625-2a06-598b-b062-9429b3437573'}, {'abstract': 'From the disco-era — “Rachael Lily Rosenbloom and Don’t You Ever Forget It!” — is getting a new turn in the spotlight at Feinstein’s/54 Below.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/09/theater/beware-a-notorious-broadway-bomb-is-ticking-again.html', 'snippet': 'From the disco-era — “Rachael Lily Rosenbloom and Don’t You Ever Forget It!” — is getting a new turn in the spotlight at Feinstein’s/54 Below.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the wall that commemorates Broadway flops at the theater district restaurant Joe Allen — a few spots down from the sinister artwork for “Carrie,” the floppiest flop of all — there hangs a stardusted poster for the 1973 musical “Rachael Lily Rosenbloom and Don’t You Ever Forget It!” To connoisseurs of stage catastrophes, that disco-infused curiosity is perched there with pedigree: It’s one of just seven Broadway musicals that closed in previews. (Can a show close if it never opened? Discuss.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 493, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 493}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 841, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1599, 'width': 1946, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10ROSENBLOOM1/10ROSENBLOOM1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beware, a Notorious Broadway Bomb Is Ticking Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beware, a Notorious Broadway Bomb Is Ticking Once More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Feinstein's/54 Below (Manhattan, NY, Music Venue)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tepper, Jennifer Ashley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Rachael Lily Rosenbloom and Don't You Ever Forget It! (Play)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jabara, Paul (1948-92)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eyen, Tom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-09T21:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96bacb9a-85ff-5a32-b24c-888172f517ef', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96bacb9a-85ff-5a32-b24c-888172f517ef'}, {'abstract': 'This western from the Dutch writer and director Martin Koolhoven puts Dakota Fanning and Guy Pearce through a gantlet of gore and gunplay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/09/movies/brimstone-review.html', 'snippet': 'This western from the Dutch writer and director Martin Koolhoven puts Dakota Fanning and Guy Pearce through a gantlet of gore and gunplay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It doesn’t take long before “Brimstone” makes good on the promise — and threat — implicit in its title. A western horror show with an unrelenting Old and New Testament kick, it opens on an ambiguous scene that soon gives way to a world of terrors, many involving children and parents. In one scene, a fetal hand juts out of the womb; in another, a man is gutted and his entrails are draped over his still-breathing body. It’s a hard, ugly world, or so the Dutch writer and director Martin Koolhoven keeps insisting for 149 grueling minutes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10BRIMSTONE/10BRIMSTONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 297, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/10BRIMSTONE/10BRIMSTONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 2