l': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/11EDO1/11EDO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/11EDO1/11EDO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/arts/11EDO1/11EDO1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Japan Had a Third Gender', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Japan Had a Third Gender', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Third Gender: Beautiful Youths in Japanese Prints (Exhibit)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Japan Society', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-10T16:05:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Chira', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chira', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9177898-4613-5cfa-afcc-c441350354e2', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9177898-4613-5cfa-afcc-c441350354e2'}, {'abstract': 'The former New York City Ballet dancer’s new work combines postmodern movement and the Higgs boson particle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/10/arts/dance/emily-coatess-incarnations-quantum-leaps-when-physics-meets-dance.html', 'snippet': 'The former New York City Ballet dancer’s new work combines postmodern movement and the Higgs boson particle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As colleagues at Yale University, the former New York City Ballet dancer Emily Coates and the particle physicist Sarah Demers have collaborated on classes, lessons and a textbook. The course they designed and taught together, “Physics of Dance,” introduced students to concepts from each discipline using the other. Taking that teamwork to the stage, Ms. Coates unveils a new work at Danspace Project starting Thursday, March 16. Part lecture, part performance, “Incarnations” addresses topics ranging from Newton’s corporeality to the Higgs boson particle. An early showing of the piece, in 2015, included a winning demonstration of how George Balanchine, who defined a high-speed American style of ballet, finessed the physics of the pirouette. Ms. Coates will be joined by Ms. Demers and the dancers Lacina Coulibaly, Iréne Hultman and Jon Kinzel — and the music director Will Orzo. The postmodern visionary Yvonne Rainer, with whom Ms. Coates has danced, will also make a brief appearance. (danspaceproject.org)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/arts/12GUIDE1/12GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1180, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/12/arts/12GUIDE1/12GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1180}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/arts/12GUIDE1/12GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 521, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/arts/12GUIDE1/12GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'h