, 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd6f777-7a24-5131-b4e2-c2ed58285837', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd6f777-7a24-5131-b4e2-c2ed58285837'}, {'abstract': 'Images of a hot-pink lake in Melbourne have taken over the internet this week. The coloration is the result of a salt-loving microbe’s attempt to make its own sunscreen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/10/science/pink-lake-westgate-melbourne-australia.html', 'snippet': 'Images of a hot-pink lake in Melbourne have taken over the internet this week. The coloration is the result of a salt-loving microbe’s attempt to make its own sunscreen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past week, the internet has been getting excited over a hot-pink lake in the heart of Melbourne. And despite what science fiction nostalgia it may conjure up, this is not a scene from “Ghostbusters” or “The X-Files.” This is very much a real, natural phenomenon that occurs all over the planet. It’s what happens when the only thing living in a supersalty lake is a single-celled, salt-loving microbe that makes pigments called carotenoids.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/07/12/science/11tb-pinklake01/11tb-pinklake01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1148, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Lake Turned Pink in Australia. It’s Not the Only One.', 'kicker': 'Trilobites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Muddy Waters: A Lake the Color of Nail Polish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Color', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lakes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALGAE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-10T18:43:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de9956ad-e979-59b3-8a41-533532bf5b6f', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de9956ad-e979-59b3-8a41-533532bf5b6f'}, {'abstract': 'The president has been noticeably less accessible since claiming, without evidence, that his predecessor had bugged Trump Tower during the campaign.', 'web_u