a Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a6f5923-6925-53dc-bdfc-37064687c668', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a6f5923-6925-53dc-bdfc-37064687c668'}, {'abstract': 'The owner of Special Operations Equipment delights in taunting his dissatisfied customers. And he says his sales increase when he does.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/10/your-money/haggler-soe-gear-consumer-complaints.html', 'snippet': 'The owner of Special Operations Equipment delights in taunting his dissatisfied customers. And he says his sales increase when he does.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few pugnacious companies out there are skeptical about the age-old maxim that the customer is always right. But the Haggler has never encountered a business that delights in calling its customers wrong, and then insults those customers in obscenity-filled emails and YouTube videos.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/12/business/12HAGGLER/12HAGGLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unhappy Customers Make Him Happy, and Rich', 'kicker': 'The Haggler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unhappy Customers Make Him Happy, and Rich', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customer Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Special Operations Equipment (Camden, Tenn, Retailer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willis, John M (1972- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-10T20:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Segal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/944875b3-c45b-58c8-abc5-1288c61c06a9', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/944875b3-c45b-58c8-abc5-1288c61c06a9'}, {'abstract': 'T’s Spotify playlist features dozens of tracks that appeared in shows in New York, London, Milan and Paris.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/10/t-magazine/fashion/fashion-week-playlist-fall-winter.html', 'snippet': 'T’s Spotify playlist features dozens of tracks that appeared in shows in New York, London, Milan and Paris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fashion month, which officially came to a close this week, saw Raf Simons’s debut at Calvin Klein — and more than a few political statements on the runway. But aside from what we saw, what we heard at the shows was especially memorable. Some soundtracks offered a direct connection to the collection they accompanied: At Chanel, for example, Elton John’s “Rocket Man” blared while a massive rocket emblazoned with the brand’s logo “took off” inside Paris’s Grand Palais. Elsewhere, soundtracks sent slightly more subliminal messages. Many designers opted for songs that were overtly political, such as David Bowie’s “This Is Not America” at Calvin Klein or Richie Havens’s “Freedom” at the Row. Many others featured music by strong female vocalists, such as Nina Simone’s “Funkier Than a Mosquito’s Tweeter” at Mara Hoffman and an all-out dance party to Christina Aguilera’s “What a Girl Wants” at Brandon Maxwell. At Ryan Roche, the designer’s 13-year-old daughter and her 15-year-old friend sang live versions of songs by Stevie Nicks in a nod to the next generation; and Stella McCartney paid tribute to the late George Michael, with models dancing to a remix of his song “Faith.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/t-magazine/10tmag-playlist/10tmag-playlist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/10/t-magazine/10tmag-playlist/10tmag-playlist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/10/t-magazine/10tmag-playlist/10tmag-playlist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': 