': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McDermid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDermid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/254b7298-4fe1-5ea7-a777-a4421a9b1051', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/254b7298-4fe1-5ea7-a777-a4421a9b1051'}, {'abstract': 'The new cookbook “Dinner With Georgia O’Keeffe” details the painter’s love affair with the kitchen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/13/dining/dinner-with-georgia-okeeffe-book-robyn-lea.html', 'snippet': 'The new cookbook “Dinner With Georgia O’Keeffe” details the painter’s love affair with the kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Dinner With Georgia O’Keeffe” chronicles the artist’s lifestyle and work through food. That it coincides with the “Georgia O’Keeffe: Living Modern” exhibition at the Brooklyn Museum is happenstance. While the show links Ms. O’Keeffe’s strong fashion sense to her art, the book goes further, adding the aesthetic of her homes and dinner table to the equation. Early on, the artist was a devotee of health foods. At a time, about 60 years ago, when the trend was hardly on the radar, she insisted on whole grains, some of which she ground herself; yogurt, which she could make; and organic ingredients. “She is quite a cook,” her husband, the photographer Alfred Stieglitz, once wrote.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/15/dining/15BURNER-BOOK/15BURNER-BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/15/dining/15BURNER-BOOK/15BURNER-BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None,