on': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'THOMAS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Maureen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'TOWEY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/fa093935-19e6-5d57-b400-33da53968ae2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/fa093935-19e6-5d57-b400-33da53968ae2'}, {'abstract': 'Danny Lyon’s photograph of prisoners toiling in a cotton field can be better understood alongside other images of labor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/14/magazine/a-photograph-never-stands-alone.html', 'snippet': 'Danny Lyon’s photograph of prisoners toiling in a cotton field can be better understood alongside other images of labor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Danny Lyon’s photograph “The Cotton Pickers” makes me tense. I love and hate it at the same time. The photograph is from the late 1960s, but its form is so iconic and its atmosphere so fabular that it could have been made a hundred years earlier. On a wide field, men are stooped over in agricultural labor. The field stretches a great distance back, ending in a line of trees that marks out the horizon. The men working the field are dressed all in white. They have long white sacks on their backs and white hats on their heads. It’s hard to tell exactly how many of them there are, perhaps just under three dozen, but the four or five in front are distinct. These men in front, in addition to being dressed similarly, are stooped in unison. Their faces are very dark, devoid of detail. It cannot be said with certainty that they are black men (they could simply be caught in deep daytime shadow), but they very likely are.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19onphoto2/19onphoto2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1317, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Photograph Never Stands Alone', 'kicker': 'On Photography', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lyon, Danny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burri, Rene (1933-2014)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-14T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teju Cole', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teju', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cole', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b0ec84f-f35d-5e50-8780-dd4744cdf9b5', 'word_count': 1434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b0ec84f-f35d-5e50-8780-dd4744cdf9b5'}, {'abstract': 'We risk obscuring real sources of violence when a tweet can be considered a tool of war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/14/magazine/if-everything-can-be-weaponized-what-should-we-fear.html', 'snippet': 'We risk obscuring real sources of violence when a tweet can be considered a tool of war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Politics is something like an art, if you ask those who practice it, and it’s something like a science, if you ask those who study it. But to the journalists who cover it, it has always been something like something else: a sport. Writing in 1968, Milton Rokeach, the social psychologist, articulated what would become a perennial complaint. “The kinds of data obtained by public-opinion research and disseminated in the mass media seem designed more to entertain than to inform,” he wrote. “The quality of the information conveyed seems not much different from that conveyed in the sports pages or, better yet, the daily racing form.” The press, especially during election years, frequently failed to exercise “journalistic conscience”; it had internalized a “racehorse philosophy.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 609, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 609}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1009, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2017, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19firstwords/19mag-19firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If Everything Can Be ‘Weaponized,’ What Should We Fear?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up in Arms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-14T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Herrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26d7d5eb-7139-5e64-9a07-48ea06225654', 'word_count': 1380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26d7d5eb-7139-5e64-9a07-48ea06225654'}, {'abstract': 'In 27 words, the Scottish novelist gives character to the little disruptors that live in our pockets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/14/magazine/new-sentences-from-serious-sweet-by-al-kennedy.html', 'snippet': 'In 27 words, the Scottish novelist gives character to the little disruptors that live in our pockets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A decade or so into the ubiquity of smartphones, our writing about them has yet to mature. We generate staggering amounts of text about their features and release dates, but not enough imagination has been applied to the more crucial thing: the way they’ve become characters in our daily lives, permanent little sprites that occupy the crevices of our clothing and time and minds. Are they more like tapeworms, hermit crabs or the birds said to clean hippos’ teeth? How do we describe the daily things they do — their high jinks and interruptions and chivalrous gestures, the way they move our minds around?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19sentences/19sentences-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19sentences/19sentences-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19sentences/19sentences-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/magazine/19sentences/19sentences-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 596, 'width': 60