erman, George', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-15T13:52:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lizette Alvarez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lizette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alvarez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca43b7a1-0bbc-5cc7-97b8-38d6fe610783', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca43b7a1-0bbc-5cc7-97b8-38d6fe610783'}, {'abstract': 'The TV Land series, featuring the comedy troupe the Katydids, serves up “Lunch Time! The Musical” for its Season 2 finale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/15/arts/television/on-teachers-its-do-as-i-say-not-as-i-sing.html', 'snippet': 'The TV Land series, featuring the comedy troupe the Katydids, serves up “Lunch Time! The Musical” for its Season 2 finale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In two seasons of their TV Land show, “Teachers,” the women of the comedy troupe the Katydids have become better and better at translating what was originally a web series into a half-hour television sitcom. But the unanswered question has been: Can they sing? The world will find out Tuesday, March 21, when “Teachers” turns to tunes for its Season 2 finale, “Lunch Time! The Musical.” In the series, the six women of the Katydids — Kate Lambert, Katie O’Brien, Kathryn Renée Thomas, Cate Freedman, Katy Colloton and Caitlin Barlow — all play wildly inappropriate teachers at an elementary school run by a bumbling principal (Tim Bagley). For the musical finale, Ms. Snap (Ms. Colloton’s character), upset that she can no longer fit into her so-called fat jeans, declares war on the unhealthy cafeteria menu to the consternation of her colleagues, who express their displeasure and more in song. So can the Katydids sing? Suffice it to say that Mr. Bagley, who gets his own ribald number, outshines them all.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/arts/19GUIDE11/19GUIDE11-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1387, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On ‘Teachers,’ It’s Do as I Say, Not as I Sing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television; Do as I Say, Not as I Sing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Teachers (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Katydids (Comedy Troupe)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TV Land', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-15T13:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db2947ee-e3b6-5834-9293-cbd8d54ca256', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db2947ee-e3b6-5834-9293-cbd8d54ca256'}, {'abstract': 'Rebecca Alban Hoffberger discusses opening the American Visionary Art Museum in Baltimore in 1995 to show the works of outsider and self-taught artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/15/arts/design/american-visionary-art-museum-rebecca-alban-hoffberger.html', 'snippet': 'Rebecca Alban Hoffberger discusses opening the American Visionary Art Museum in Baltimore in 1995 to show the works of outsider and self-taught artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an age when few individuals can create an institution as complex and expensive as a major museum, Rebecca Alban Hoffberger has succeeded in giving the art world something unexpected: a full-scale urban museum featuring outsider and self-taught artists. The American Visionary Art Museum, or AVAM, which she opened in Baltimore in 1995 and which she still directs, quickly became one of the city’s most popular cultural institutions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/07/arts/16CONVERSATION1/16CONVERSATION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/07/arts/16CONVERSATION1/16CONVERSATION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, '