Kore-eda’s latest family drama, focusing on a writer who’s fallen on hard times, works in a quiet cinematic register.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/15/movies/after-the-storm-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Japanese director Hirokazu Kore-eda’s latest family drama, focusing on a writer who’s fallen on hard times, works in a quiet cinematic register.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Japanese actor Hiroshi Abe has one of the great hangdog faces in cinema today. At least that’s the case in “After the Storm,” a new film from the writer-director Hirokazu Kore-eda. Tall and lean, Mr. Abe has handsome features — floppy dark hair across his forehead, prominent cheekbones and a long jaw line. But here, playing Ryota Shinoda, a onetime literary sensation fallen on hard times professionally and personally, he also has lines around and bags under his eyes, a perpetual five o’clock shadow and a downcast mouth. He looks as if he could be trouble.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/17/arts/17AFTERSTROM/17AFTERSTROM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/17/arts/17AFTERSTROM/17AFTERSTROM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/17/arts/17AFTERSTROM/17AFTERSTROM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/17/arts/17AFTERSTROM/17AFTERSTROM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1108, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url