24db2db'}, {'abstract': 'Judge Gorsuch’s hearing next week will test whether the modern confirmation process can succeed in eliciting a nominee’s views. Here are a few questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/16/opinion/a-time-to-choose-for-the-supreme-court.html', 'snippet': 'Judge Gorsuch’s hearing next week will test whether the modern confirmation process can succeed in eliciting a nominee’s views. Here are a few questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Members of the Senate Judiciary Committee are getting plenty of advice in the run-up to next week’s Supreme Court confirmation hearing for Judge Neil M. Gorsuch. I offered my own advice in the days immediately following Judge Gorsuch’s nomination: Senators should ask how he would have decided Supreme Court cases from the past. Don’t accept the standard nominee response that “I can’t answer because that question might come before the court.” It has already come before the court. (This suggestion is based on a 2006 article by two Yale law professors, Robert Post and Reva Siegel.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/opinion/16greenhouseWeb/15greenhouseWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/16/opinion/16greenhouseWeb/15greenhouseWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/opinion/16greenhouseWeb/15greenhouseWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/opinion/16greenhouseWeb/15greenhouseWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name