': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/fcc4eb9b-2df8-53b8-9838-a30e6ebd5c9d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/fcc4eb9b-2df8-53b8-9838-a30e6ebd5c9d'}, {'abstract': 'The rowdy, six-day long Busojaras Festival, taking place two hours from Budapest, welcomes spring with a bang — and a bonfire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/16/t-magazine/busojaras-festival-hungary-lent-travel.html', 'snippet': 'The rowdy, six-day long Busojaras Festival, taking place two hours from Budapest, welcomes spring with a bang — and a bonfire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent Sunday morning in Mohacs, Hungary, a buffet was set out in the parking lot of an apartment building. But more lavish than the pastries and meats on display was the attire of those eating them: Men wore oversize shearling coats with cowbells chained around their waists, and women lifted lace hanging from black eye masks to have a bite. Hand-carved horned masks and maces were propped neatly against a wall and a taxidermied fox was tied to a pitchfork in such a way that it seemed to be giving the middle finger to everyone in the vicinity. The men and women were members of the Kecskeszarv Busocsoport (Goat Horn Buso Group), and they gathered to prepare for another day in the six-day-long Busojaras Festival, which culminates each year on the Tuesday before Lent.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/13/t-magazine/13tmag-mohacs-slide-ZGD5/13tmag-mohacs-slide-ZGD5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/13/t-magazine/13tmag-mohacs-slide-ZGD5/13tmag-mohacs-slide-ZGD5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnai