section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Crosson Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': 'Crosson', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02449dfb-54a3-59b6-bbdc-3aba27878393', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02449dfb-54a3-59b6-bbdc-3aba27878393'}, {'abstract': 'Will the March for Science help or hurt scientists and scientific evidence?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/21/learning/what-is-your-opinion-on-the-march-for-science.html', 'snippet': 'Will the March for Science help or hurt scientists and scientific evidence?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Study the map above, or a larger version here. The orange circles indicate sites all over the world where satellite marches for the March for Science will be held on Earth Day, April 22.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-articleLarge.png', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-jumbo.png', 'height': 502, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/21/learning/lesson-plans/21SOLN-marchmap/21SOLN-marchmap-superJumbo.png', 'height': 502, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is Your Opinion on the March for Science?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-03-21T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Crosson Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': 'Crosson', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dc04572-266a-5cdb-9c58-797da5245a84', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dc04572-266a-5cdb-9c58-797da5245a84'}, {'abstract': 'In 23 words, the writer infuses grammar with purpose.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/21/magazine/new-sentences-from-reticent-sonnet-by-anne-carson.html', 'snippet': 'In 23 words, the writer infuses grammar with purpose.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Never underestimate a comma. A comma can do anything. It can divvy up your most complex thoughts as precisely as a butcher cleaves a cow into prime and subprime cuts of beef. It can be a paper bag that allows your hyperventilating mind to breathe. Commas are the nails, rivets, screws, joints, braces, brackets, hinges, pins and pegs in the vast architecture of human thought. Lose one, and the whole structure will threaten to rattle apart. Hazards abound. A sentence with a missing comma is a horse trailer coming unhitched on a highway at 70 miles per hour. A sentence with an extra comma is a boulder in your swimming pool.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 642}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26sentences/26mag-26sentences-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Sentences: From ‘Reticent Sonnet,’ by Anne Carson', 'kicker': 'New Sentences', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From ‘Reticent Sonnet,’ by Anne Carson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Anne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-21T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c97a889-4eae-5091-94e0-05ac1341fac2', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c97a889-4eae-5091-94e0-05ac1341fac2'}, {'abstract': 'When Americans start accusing one another of violating the nation’s spirit, it’s a good sign we can’t agree on what that spirit even is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/21/magazine/how-un-american-became-the-political-insult-of-the-moment.html', 'snippet': 'When Americans start accusing one another of violating the nation’s spirit, it’s a good sign we can’t agree on what that spirit even is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Conservatives have long complained that “I don’t recognize my country anymore.” In Trump’s America, though, it’s liberals who see something insufficiently, well, American. Issues once assumed to be settled — the desirability of racial tolerance, a general preference for democratic processes — now appear, suddenly and ominously, to be up for debate. For many, this has created a sense of rupture, a disconnect between what they had assumed would be happening at this moment in history and what is actually taking place — as if the 2016 election shifted the nation into some dystopian reality that was never meant to be.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 591}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1008, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1470, 'width': 1493, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26firstwords/26mag-26firstwords-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How ‘Un-American’ Became the Political Insult of the Moment', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Second Nature', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-21T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beverly Gage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beverly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30be43b6-4de2-5422-b92a-cf30814774a0', 'word_count': 1497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30be43b6-4de2-5422-b92a-cf30814774a0'}, {'abstract': 'With enough success, these businesses go from enabling transactions to controlling entire economies. But these raised stakes also create tensions that are becoming harder to ignore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/21/magazine/platform-companies-are-becoming-more-powerful-but-what-exactly-do-they-want.html', 'snippet': 'With enough success, these businesses go from enabling transactions to controlling entire economies. But these raised stakes also create tensions that are becoming harder to ignore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During a February ride in San Francisco, Travis Kalanick, the chief executive of Uber, was recorded arguing with and eventually berating an Uber driver from the back seat of his car. The driver, who had been working with the company since 2011, accused Kalanick of undercutting drivers of high-end cars like his, plunging him into bankruptcy. Kalanick responded with a lecture about the basic economic logic of his company: Soon, the supply of luxury cars on the app would be reduced, causing demand to increase. Besides, he went on, if the company hadn’t added a lower-priced tier, it would have been beaten by competitors. This did not satisfy the driver, which seemed to enrage Kalanick, who erupted into a moralizing tirade. “Some people don’t like to take responsibility for their own [expletive],” he said, before leaving the car.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 502}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 856, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/magazine/26onmoney1/26mag-26onmoney-t_CA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 1635, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Platform Companies Are Becoming More Powerful — but What Exactly Do They Want?', 'kicker': 'On Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Platform Companies Are Becoming More Powerful — but What Exactly Do They Want?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Uber Technologies Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Airbnb', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TaskRabbit Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiverr International Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kalanick, Travis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Matchmakers: The New Economics of Multisided Platforms (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-21T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Herrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ab0befd-4460-5a16-9c58-af8e8816b5d8', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ab0befd-4460-5a16-9c58-af8e8816b5d8'}, {'abstract': 'A graphic novel, a story collection, an apocalyptic novella and New York City under water: N.K. Jemisin reviews the latest in science fiction and fantasy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/21/books/review/the-best-of-new-sci-fi-and-fantasy-from-an-intergalactic-love-story-to-new-york-city-under-water.html', 'snippet': 'A graphic novel, a story collection, an apocalyptic novella and New York City under water: N.K. Jemisin reviews the latest in science fiction and fantasy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s an ancient story: Two galaxy-spanning civilizations, alike in dignity, have been embroiled in a proxy war for centuries. With multiple factions happily profiteering from the conflict, the appearance of star-crossed lovers — who inherently symbolize peace — represents a dire threat. The audacity of the “Saga” series of graphic novels, written by Brian K. Vaughan and illustrated by Fiona Staples, lies first in its allegorical examination of who fears “miscegenation” and why, and second in its defiance of the usual conventions. Here, the doomed lovers behold the forces arrayed against them and basically declare, “Not today, you don’t.” This saga is of their survival.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/books/review/0326-BKS-Otherworldly/0326-BKS-Otherworldly-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/books/review/0326-BKS-Otherworldly/0326-BKS-Otherworldly-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 898}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/books/review/0326-BKS-Otherworldly/0326-BKS-Otherworldly-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/books/review/0326-BKS-Otherworldly/0326-BKS-Otherworldly-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1659, 'width': 1109, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/books/review/0326-BKS-Otherworldly/0326-BKS-Otherworldly-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/26/books/review/0326-BKS-Otherworldly/0326-BKS-Otherworldly-thumbSta