17:16:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b14c1dc-ed34-52b7-b9ae-94f76b1bcd2c', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b14c1dc-ed34-52b7-b9ae-94f76b1bcd2c'}, {'abstract': 'The new executive chef at Benoit in New York, Laetitia Rouabah, is teaming up with Hometown Bar-B-Que’s pitmaster, Billy Durney.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/21/dining/benoit-hometown-bar-b-q.html', 'snippet': 'The new executive chef at Benoit in New York, Laetitia Rouabah, is teaming up with Hometown Bar-B-Que’s pitmaster, Billy Durney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Smoked beef cheeks with a French accent: That’s what Laetitia Rouabah, the new chef at Benoit in Manhattan, is cooking under the tutelage of Billy Durney, who owns Hometown Bar-B-Que in Red Hook, Brooklyn. Last week, the two were doing what the pitmaster described as “messing with French food.” The result will be a series of dinners at Benoit. Alain Ducasse, the owner of the bistro and a fan of Hometown, got Ms. Rouabah interested in barbecue, and she banked on it as a way to give the French bistro some American flavor. “But I can give the barbecue more finesse,” she said. There will be a Bourguignon sauce for those beef cheeks, and barbecue rub on the deviled eggs: Le Cochon & Company dinners, Tuesdays in April, $95 per person, Benoit, 60 West 55th Street, 646-943-7373, benoitny.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/22/dining/22BURNER-COCHON/22BURNER-COCHON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600,