 side, but after my second child was born in 2007 with severe disabilities it was impossible to go back to TV work; the hours were too long and I needed to be there for him. However, when he was 3 I went back to school for an M.F.A. in film production and to make a documentary about our family’s experience of having a child with special needs. It’s called “Softening”; a short version of it titled “My Brother, Teddy” became an Op-Doc. The experience renewed my interest in making work about family; at least, it affirmed the value of it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Does Life Live?', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Softening (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-21T19:41:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By KELLY O'BRIEN", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'BRIEN", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7203e4c-0be9-5ab9-989d-27d295fa3fdc', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7203e4c-0be9-5ab9-989d-27d295fa3fdc'}, {'abstract': '“Where does the sun go? Can girls be robots? Why don’t worms have faces?” A little girl wants to know.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/opinion/100000004976604/how-does-life-live.html', 'snippet': '“Where does the sun go? Can girls be robots? Why don’t worms have faces?” A little girl wants to know.', '