ted', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2859fa33-c2eb-5cf0-8dc3-9b4b4f46b191', 'word_count': 1427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2859fa33-c2eb-5cf0-8dc3-9b4b4f46b191'}, {'abstract': 'This show is set in the days when one in five British women worked in prostitution. It is boldly female in episodes created by women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/22/arts/television/harlots-on-hulu-has-sex-but-settle-down-guys.html', 'snippet': 'This show is set in the days when one in five British women worked in prostitution. It is boldly female in episodes created by women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let the bodices rip, “Downton Abbey” zealots: Lady Sybil is now a whore. Or rather, the actress Jessica Brown Findlay is portraying one in “Harlots,” debuting Wednesday, March 29, on Hulu. Set in Georgian England — when, historians estimate, as many as one in five women worked in the sex trade — the series stars Samantha Morton as Margaret Wells, a London brothel owner; Ms. Brown Findlay as Charlotte, her older daughter and the city’s courtesan nonpareil; Eloise Smyth as Lucy, her younger, whose virginity she intends to auction off to the highest bidder; and Lesley Manville as Lydia Quigley, her rival madam.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26GUIDE2/2