T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/fe15c566-d7b7-5098-85ba-2b3368e1fa06', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/fe15c566-d7b7-5098-85ba-2b3368e1fa06'}, {'abstract': 'At home in Copenhagen, the Danish designer Oliver Gustav takes his aesthetic to a moody extreme.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/22/t-magazine/oliver-gustav-interior-designer-copenhagen.html', 'snippet': 'At home in Copenhagen, the Danish designer Oliver Gustav takes his aesthetic to a moody extreme.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the paintings of the 19th-century Danish artist Vilhelm Hammershoi, the color gray achieves a beguiling incandescence. His introspective interiors, which feature the simple, classical architecture of 18th-century Copenhagen apartments bathed in Scandinavia’s soft northern light, present havens of timeless tranquillity. Entering Oliver Gustav’s home is not unlike stepping into one of Hammershoi’s paintings. For one thing, it is in a building quite similar to Strandgade 30, where the painter and his wife lived for 10 years, and which served as a setting for his most important works. But it’s also the stillness and the way the light softens the sharp angles of each room.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '161', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/15/t-magazine/15tmag-gustav-slide-QXK4/15tmag-gustav-slide-QXK4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/15/t-magazine/15tmag-gustav-slide-QXK4/15tmag-gustav-slide-QXK4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/15/t-magazine/15tmag-gustav-slide-QXK4/15tmag-gustav-slide-QXK4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 