': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d67cfbf-16d8-5a86-97cd-fd006389540f', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d67cfbf-16d8-5a86-97cd-fd006389540f'}, {'abstract': 'Education Secretary Betsy DeVos seems poised to undo rules protecting students from taking on excessive debt for useless degrees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/23/opinion/predator-colleges-may-thrive-again.html', 'snippet': 'Education Secretary Betsy DeVos seems poised to undo rules protecting students from taking on excessive debt for useless degrees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Congress has tried since the 1940s to curb predatory for-profit schools that survive almost solely on federal money while they saddle students with crushing loans for useless degrees. As the industry’s scandals grew and its role in the student debt crisis became more excessive, the Obama administration established rules that could get the worst of these programs off the federal dole. But the Education Department under its new secretary, Betsy DeVos, seems ready to undermine those regulations and let predatory schools flourish once again.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/opinion/23thu2/23thu2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/23/opinion/23thu2/23thu2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/opinion/23thu2/23thu2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1022, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/opinion/23thu2/23thu2