llow for a direct popular vote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — For the fifth time in the two decades since this former British colony’s return to Chinese rule, Hong Kong’s next chief executive will be selected on Sunday by a committee stacked with supporters of the Chinese government rather than by a free election.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/world/24Hongkong-1/24Hongkong-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Hong Kong Chooses Its Next Leader, China Still Pulls the Strings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Hong Kong Chooses Its Next Leader, China Is Still Pulling the Strings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lam, Carrie (1957- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsang, John (1951- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-23T11:45:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4315825-10e8-5ee1-8ff3-c06801a967cb', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4315825-10e8-5ee1-8ff3-c06801a967cb'}, {'abstract': '“Power” at Sprüth Magers Los Angeles and “We Wanted a Revolution” at the Brooklyn Museum explore themes of feminism, womanism, race and gender equality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/23/t-magazine/art/black-women-artists-brooklyn-museum-spruth-magers-los-angeles.html', 'snippet': '“Power” at Sprüth Magers Los Angeles and “We Wanted a Revolution” at the Brooklyn Museum explore themes of feminism, womanism, race and gender equality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two upcoming shows, one on each coast, will soon celebrate the myriad contributions of black women to the landscape of American art. “Power,” curated by Todd Levin at Sprüth Magers Los Angeles, is a major survey of work from the 19th century to the present, and includes pieces by Faith Ringgold, Mickalene Thomas and Kara Walker. The exhibition, titled after a 1970 gospel song, considers art as a means of resistance, whether in a pointed way — as in the case of contemporary photographer Nona Faustine’s nude self-portraits, meant to recall the slave trade — or merely by existing in the face of oppression, as is true of Alma Thomas’s ’60s-era abstract paintings.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 521}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 890, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/23/t-magazine/23tmag-rev1/23tmag-rev1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 1