 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Caro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5c04c64-c3be-5c7a-a564-e3e1d1126c12', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5c04c64-c3be-5c7a-a564-e3e1d1126c12'}, {'abstract': 'No matter who becomes Hong Kong’s new leader, one faction will come out on top — and it won’t be Beijing’s proxies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/opinion/for-some-a-win-win-election-in-hong-kong.html', 'snippet': 'No matter who becomes Hong Kong’s new leader, one faction will come out on top — and it won’t be Beijing’s proxies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hong Kong’s new leader will be selected on Sunday. Since Britain handed the territory back to China in 1997, the election of the chief executive — which is performed by a special 1,200-member committee stacked in Beijing’s favor — has generally been a foregone conclusion. But this year China’s leaders may not be able to dictate the outcome. A faction of powerful local business interests sidelined during the last election, in 2012, will come out on top no matter the result of the vote itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25lian-inyt/25lian-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25lian-inyt/25lian-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25lian-inyt/25lian-inyt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 