 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-24T16:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Chappatte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chappatte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/de747f67-1640-58b0-99f1-69b54d4679c3', 'word_count': 15, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de747f67-1640-58b0-99f1-69b54d4679c3'}, {'abstract': "A by-no-means exhaustive list of great things we're stumbled upon.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/t-magazine/5-things-t-editors-are-really-into-right-now.html', 'snippet': "A by-no-means exhaustive list of great things we're stumbled upon.", 'lead_paragraph': 'When visiting Philly, the waterfront working-class neighborhood of Fishtown may not be at the top of your list. Not just because you likely haven’t heard of it, but because there isn’t anywhere to stay. But it now comes highly recommended by locals for its growing food scene and small-town vibes despite steady gentrification. It’s home to more than a few hipster and creative types, and on one quiet corner sits a charming restaurant to rival any Brooklyn eatery: Wm. Mulherin’s Sons, which gets its name from the 19th-century whisky bottling and blending company that once occupied the space. Nearly every ingredient on its menu is made in-house, right down to the flour blends for the homemade pizza crusts. (Bon Appétit named it one of the 50 best restaurants to open in 2016.) And now, you can sleep upstairs — in one of the four chic rooms Wm. Mulherin’s Sons just opened as a hotel. They’re uniquely designed (and stocked with things like Kinfolk magazine and Aesop toiletries), and perfectly sized for longer stays (with full kitchens and even washing machines). The rooms are conveniently located above Wm. Mulherin’s Sons (for nightcaps) and around the corner from the La Colombe flagship (for morning coffee.) The most exciting part of your stay might just be checking in: Like at any old seaside pub, keys are simply doled out by anyone working the bar, so you can grab a pint before heading up to your room. From $275/night, wmmulherinssons.com —\xa0ALAINNA LEXIE BEDDIE', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/24/t-magazine/travel/24tmag-philly-01/24tmag-philly-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '5 Things T Editors Are Really Into Right Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salad Dressings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Ulla', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-24T16:54:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/661d78eb-90ac-5c8a-a1e6-6ff012334a70', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/661d78eb-90ac-5c8a-a1e6-6ff012334a70'}, {'abstract': 'Firm-fleshed and lean, monkfish was once known as poor man’s lobster.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/dining/monkfish-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'Firm-fleshed and lean, monkfish was once known as poor man’s lobster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The monkfish ranks among the most fierce-looking of sea creatures, with an ugly mug only a monkfish’s mother could love. The horrid face with its wide, round smile reveals two enormous bear-trap-like rows of razor-sharp teeth pointing backward. This presents a clear hazard for unwary small fish that happen to be in proximity when said monkfish has its mouth open. They swim in naïvely but can’t swim out.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/29/dining/29KITCHEN1/29KITCHEN1-articleLarge-v2.j