 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Lyman and Joanna Berendt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berendt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5cd0bfc-095d-5f21-81f9-ac38741f072d', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5cd0bfc-095d-5f21-81f9-ac38741f072d'}, {'abstract': 'The climber Royal Robbins, who died recently, was a pioneer on Yosemite’s big walls, where he practiced a minimalist, spiritual approach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/opinion/on-the-rock-looking-inward-and-outward.html', 'snippet': 'The climber Royal Robbins, who died recently, was a pioneer on Yosemite’s big walls, where he practiced a minimalist, spiritual approach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeffersonville, Vt. — Just at the edge of sight, a man dangled from a rope, high in the air. For days, storms had dimmed the sky, but the angle of the cliff overhung so much that the drops of water fell to the ground without touching him. It was as if, he later wrote, he climbed under the protection of a “stupendous umbrella.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25sporting1Web/25sporting1Web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Rock, Looking Inward and Outward', 'kicker': 'Sporting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Climbing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robbins, Royal (1935-2017)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yosemite National Park (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-24T23:32:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Ives', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/40044e44-28ef-5abe-8e8b-b57de78b9454', 'word_count': 996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40044e44-28ef-5abe-8e8b-b57de78b9454'}, {'abstract': 'We need to be budgeting to defend against the next pandemic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/opinion/the-real-threat-to-national-security-deadly-disease.html', 'snippet': 'We need to be budgeting to defend against the next pandemic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the Trump administration is proposing significantly increased military spending to enhance our national security, it seems to have lost sight of the greatest national security threat of all: our fight against infectious disease.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1800, 'width': 1800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25osterholm/25osterholm-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Real Threat to National Security: Deadly Disease', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Microbe Wars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institutes of Health', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Prevention and Public Health Fund', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-24T23:32:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael T. Osterholm and Mark Olshaker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Osterholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Olshaker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9020bf92-09b4-57f8-926d-1d56f7763400', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9020bf92-09b4-57f8-926d-1d56f7763400'}, {'abstract': 'Understanding how she got swept up in evil has been a lifelong question for me.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/opinion/i-loved-my-grandmother-but-she-was-a-nazi.html', 'snippet': 'Understanding how she got swept up in evil has been a lifelong question for me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My grandparents were Nazis. It took me until recently to be able to say — or write — this. I used to think of and refer to them as “ordinary Germans,” as if that was a distinct and morally neutral category. But like many “ordinary Germans,” they were members of the Nazi Party — they joined in 1937.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/25/opinion/25shattuckWweb/25shattuckWweb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1391, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Loved My Grandmother. But She Was a Nazi.', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Grandmother Was a Nazi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grandparents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-24T23:32:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdac80bc-1772-5b86-b0f8-bb2a2f135120', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdac80bc-1772-5b86-b0f8-bb2a2f135120'}, {'abstract': 'The F.B.I. investigation into possible Trump campaign coordination with Russia and claims by the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee raise questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/24/us/politics/primer-on-surveillance-practices-and-privacy.html', 'snippet': 'The F.B.I. investigation into possible Trump campaign coordination with Russia and claims by the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee raise questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The assertion by the Republican chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, Representative Devin Nunes, that he saw intelligence reports showing that government surveillance programs “incidentally” collected communications involving Trump transition team members between November and January, has further muddied an already murky imbroglio.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'cr