news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Forman Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Forman', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f420779-8b41-54f7-9244-f5c809a859cd', 'word_count': 2148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f420779-8b41-54f7-9244-f5c809a859cd'}, {'abstract': 'Our most successful cities are conspiracies against the public good.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/25/opinion/sunday/break-up-the-liberal-city.html', 'snippet': 'Our most successful cities are conspiracies against the public good.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE age of Trump has inspired soul-searching within our overclass — long nights reading “Hillbilly Elegy,” mostly — but also a wave of cosmopolitan pride. During the presidential campaign, when Trump talked about making America great again, Hillary Clinton countered that “America is already great” — meaning, of course, dynamic and diverse and tolerant and future-oriented, all the things that Trump seems to dismiss and his voters seem to fear.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/sunday-review/26Douthat/26Douthat-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/sunday-review/26Douthat/26Douthat-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/sunday-review/26Douthat/26Douthat-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'