', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aa37f3f-8783-55cb-a252-2081b96c9ef1', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aa37f3f-8783-55cb-a252-2081b96c9ef1'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on Monday, March 27, 2017.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/26/pageoneplus/corrections-march-27-2017.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on Monday, March 27, 2017.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INTERNATIONAL', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: March 27, 2017', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-03-27T02:57:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/d97ccce7-4fb3-5457-a7e0-2335c8a46d68', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d97ccce7-4fb3-5457-a7e0-2335c8a46d68'}, {'abstract': '“Baby Jane” wraps up, and the tension sets in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/26/arts/television/feud-bette-and-joan-more-or-less-recap.html', 'snippet': '“Baby Jane” wraps up, and the tension sets in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After “Baby Jane” wrapped, Bette Davis — in anticipatory panic that the film would bomb, and an equally intense panic that she would be pilloried for her broad characterization in the film — placed an ad in “Variety” with the heading “Situation Wanted.” The ad read, in part: “Thirty years experience as an actress in motion pictures. Mobile still and more affable than rumor would have it. Wants steady employment in Hollywood. (Has had Broadway.) References upon request.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26feud/26feud-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Feud: Bette and Joan’ Episode 4 Recap: Aftermath', 'kicker': 'Feud', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Feud: Bette and Joan (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-27T03:01:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': "By Sheila O'Malley", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Malley", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/09c26d7f-ed12-55f6-ae31-bb42edd8b3eb', 'word_count': 1167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09c26d7f-ed12-55f6-ae31-bb42edd8b3eb'}, {'abstract': 'Chuck’s wife negotiates a deal to help ease her husband’s burdens. So why does Chuck feel so bad?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/26/arts/television/billions-season-indian-four-recap.html', 'snippet': 'Chuck’s wife negotiates a deal to help ease her husband’s burdens. So why does Chuck feel so bad?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two weeks ago, I wrote about Wendy Rhoades’s period in exile, and how it has affected both her as a character and a show that relies on her as a unifying element. Her time as a free agent had left Chuck and Axe without the woman they claimed but could not possess, and her re-entry into their lives rekindles a conflict that circumstances had miraculously conspired to resolve. “Billions” isn’t a exactly comedy, but the psychology it assigns to these two Masters-of-the-Universe types emphasizes the absurd gulf between their intellectual capacity and their raw, bone-stupid masculinity. They may sip on fancy scotch or take the private helicopter to dinner, but at heart they’re possessive and tempestuous beasts, like Jake LaMotta in expensive shoes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/26/arts/26billions/26billions-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Billions’ Season 2, Episode 6 Recap: Wendy’s Back', 'kicker': 'Billions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Billions (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-27T03:01:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Tobias', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tobias', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2545f81d-ff46-5f34-bb59-79898127e26a', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2545f81d-ff46-5f34-bb59-79898127e26a'}, {'abstract': 'Prosecutors requested a warrant for Ms. Park, who was removed from office as South Korea’s president this month, on charges of bribery and abuse of power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/2