 massively underestimating how hard tax reform is going to be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Republican Health Care bill failed because it was a bad bill that had almost no authentic public support. It took benefits away from tens of millions of vulnerable people in order to give tax breaks to the rich few.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/28/opinion/28brooksWeb/28brooksWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Elephants Learn From Failure?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can Elephants Learn From Failure?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Value-Added Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-28T07:21:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a40372a-3e0a-506a-9837-0f232654a071', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a40372a-3e0a-506a-9837-0f232654a071'}, {'abstract': 'Aida Muluneh’s colorful photos of figures in decorative body paint explore issues of African identity, women and the connection between heritage and homeland.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/03/28/exploring-ethiopias-past-and-future-through-body-painting/', 'snippet': 'Aida Muluneh’s colorful photos of figures in decorative body paint explore issues of African identity, women and the connection between heritage and homeland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aida Muluneh spent a peripatetic childhood in Yemen, England, Cyprus, Canada and the United States before settling in the States to study and become a photojournalist. She returned to Ethiopia, the land of her birth, about a decade ago, where her work addresses issues of women, African identity and the connection between heritage and homeland. Her photos feature decorative body paintings that reflect Ethiopian culture or traditional fabrics and baskets and reflect her own life’s journey.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Ethiopia’s Past and Future Through Body Painting', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-03-28T08:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By SARAH MOROZ', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MOROZ', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a69e951-1b2b-5edd-b4cd-73415964298d', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a69e951-1b2b-5edd-b4cd-73415964298d'}, {'abstract': 'The canceling of the vote by Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull signaled that concerns over China’s human rights record would continue to limit the two countries’ ties.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/28/world/australia/malcolm-turnbull-china-extradition-treaty.html', 'snippet': 'The canceling of the vote by Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull signaled that concerns over China’s human rights record would continue to limit the two countries’ ties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — Prime Minister Malcolm Turnbull canceled a parliamentary vote to ratify an extradition treaty with China on Tuesday after opposition lawmakers said they would not support it, and after some members of Mr. Turnbull’s own Liberal Party expressed concern about moving forward.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/29/world/29oztreaty-1/29oztreaty-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 418, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/29/world/29oztreaty-1/29oztreaty-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 418}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt