 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turnbull, Malcolm (1954- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sydney (Australia)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-28T08:43:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Australia', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e62751c-f62a-5a14-9e44-6c501c353271', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e62751c-f62a-5a14-9e44-6c501c353271'}, {'abstract': 'In the game’s infancy, different leagues played by different rules — and that cacophony encouraged creativity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/28/magazine/as-baseball-considers-change-it-should-look-to-its-past.html', 'snippet': 'In the game’s infancy, different leagues played by different rules — and that cacophony encouraged creativity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1857, 14 baseball clubs met at Smith’s Hotel on Broome Street in Manhattan to draft a new set of rules and regulations. In an effort to make the game more manly and scientific, members of the Knickerbocker Club, New York’s most influential team, proposed that “the ball must weigh not less than 6 nor more than 6¼ ounces avoirdupois” and that “no person shall be permitted to approach or to speak with the referee, umpires or players, or in any manner to interrupt or interfere during the progress of the game.” Most significant was the rule that changed the terms of victory. The winner was no longer the first team to score 21 runs, but the one that accrued the most runs after the end of nine innings. The reasoning behind the change was simple and logical: Fielding, which had been a stone-handed disaster during baseball’s earliest days, had improved rapidly, making it hard for any one team to reach 21 before the sun went down. The modern nine-inning game was one of the first official attempts to make baseball games go faster.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 699}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 879, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1758, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02onsports1-copy/02mag-02onsports-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Baseball Considers Change, It Should Look to Its Past', 'kicker': 'On Sports', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Knickerbocker Club', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-28T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Caspian Kang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': 'Caspian', 'lastname': 'Kang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a9c340e-617e-50ee-824b-686fc431d6a8', 'word_count': 1357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a9c340e-617e-50ee-824b-686fc431d6a8'}, {'abstract': 'Online, where information is power, we’re all at risk of being exposed as vulnerable, ignorant or worse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/28/magazine/none-of-us-are-safe-from-getting-owned.html', 'snippet': 'Online, where information is power, we’re all at risk of being exposed as vulnerable, ignorant or worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the escalating rhetoric of public shaming, being embarrassed online is tantamount to being wiped from the face of the earth. Whenever a late-night host upbraids a public figure in a monologue or a pundit bests another in a Twitter fight, onlookers crowd around to declare the loser DESTROYED! or EVISCERATED! or ETHERED! or ANNIHILATED! But alongside these symbols of destruction has risen another, more apt metaphor for the dynamics of the modern media power play. In this one, the defeated party wasn’t killed, but possessed: They got OWNED.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subty