t_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Landon Thomas Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Landon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbad0238-a543-5f08-9c98-920b5d2bda01', 'word_count': 1099, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbad0238-a543-5f08-9c98-920b5d2bda01'}, {'abstract': 'The SITI Company’s opaque, mystifying play is built entirely from the composer’s quotations, arranged into an exploration of the artistic process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/theater/review-in-chess-match-no-5-the-words-of-john-cage-deconstructed.html', 'snippet': 'The SITI Company’s opaque, mystifying play is built entirely from the composer’s quotations, arranged into an exploration of the artistic process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades, Marcel Duchamp was busy “conspicuously playing chess, not visibly doing art.” Much the same happens in “Chess Match No. 5,” which quotes that remark by the composer John Cage about the conceptualist. The two people onstage are conspicuously playing chess; they also make toast, fiddle with a radio, drink tea and trade disconnected aphorisms and anecdotes. They are not visibly doing theater, if that means plot, traditional characters or singing cats.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/30/arts/30CHESSMATCH1/29CHESSMATCH1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Chess Match No. 5,’ the Words of John Cage, Deconstructed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cerebral Peek  Into an Artist’s Mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Chess Match No 5 (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SITI Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bogart, Anne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cage, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarke, Jocelyn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-28T21:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Vincentelli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vincent