ocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ginsburg, Ruth Bader', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-29T07:21:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a2b06b7-79e0-5eae-9572-dde83a5a5912', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a2b06b7-79e0-5eae-9572-dde83a5a5912'}, {'abstract': 'President Trump might soon sign a bill that lets internet service providers sell your web browsing history and other personal information.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/29/opinion/how-the-republicans-sold-your-privacy-to-internet-providers.html', 'snippet': 'President Trump might soon sign a bill that lets internet service providers sell your web browsing history and other personal information.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday afternoon, while most people were focused on the latest news from the House Intelligence Committee, the House quietly voted to undo rules that keep internet service providers — the companies like Comcast, Verizon and Charter that you pay for online access — from selling your personal information.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/29/opinion/29wheeler/29wheeler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/29/opinion/29wheeler/29wheeler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/29/opinion/29wheeler/29wheeler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub