 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-30T08:40:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Ives', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/541a86bc-ca06-53ae-bf26-301e366ed139', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/541a86bc-ca06-53ae-bf26-301e366ed139'}, {'abstract': 'Marilyn Stasio’s crime column takes us to three watery venues — Venice, the English Fenland and the French Riviera. And for a break, how about chilly Norway?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/books/review/earthly-remains-donna-leon.html', 'snippet': 'Marilyn Stasio’s crime column takes us to three watery venues — Venice, the English Fenland and the French Riviera. And for a break, how about chilly Norway?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When she’s writing about her beloved Venice, Donna Leon can do no wrong. And EARTHLY REMAINS (Atlantic Monthly, $25), her new mystery featuring Commissario Guido Brunetti, is one of her best. It’s also one of her saddest, dealing as it does with the seemingly unstoppable polluting of the great lagoon. “We’ve poisoned it all, killed it all,” mourns Davide Casati, the aged caretaker of the house on the island of Sant’Erasmo where Brunetti is taking a medical leave for job-induced stress.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 874, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 874}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 704, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Crime/0402-BKS-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1407, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Best and Latest in Crime Fiction', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death by Venice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Earthly Remains (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leon, Donna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Loving Husband (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kent, Christobel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Fifth Element (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brekke, Jorgen', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Prussian Blue (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerr, Philip', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-30T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e407b1f-cff5-5634-bd79-74e39069d619', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e407b1f-cff5-5634-bd79-74e39069d619'}, {'abstract': 'Jim Harrison’s appetite was legendary. His recipe ought to be, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/magazine/a-larger-than-life-caribbean-stew.html', 'snippet': 'Jim Harrison’s appetite was legendary. His recipe ought to be, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was staying in a square cinder-block house on a dirt road outside the Lovely Bay settlement on Acklins Island in the southern Bahamas. This was a number of years ago, a fishing trip, long days spent out on the flats of the Bight of Acklins with a local guide named Fidel Johnson. Each night we returned to eat fiery stews prepared by his sister, Doramae Johnson. They were bright crimson affairs, perhaps familiar to those who have spent time eating in the islands of the Bahamas and Lesser Antilles. Doramae ginned them up from canned goods and fresh conchs, lobster, sometimes chicken or pork shipped in from Nassau, and served them on a small table in her brother’s kitchen. They were bewilderingly delicious. I begged her for a recipe. She only smiled. I tried to come off the water early to cook with her. She was onto me. The stew was finished, left warm in the oven, and she was gone, her secrets kept.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02eat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02eat-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02eat-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02mag-02eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02mag-02eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02mag-02eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02eat-copy/02mag-02eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Larger-Than-Life Caribbean Stew', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Larger Than Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bahama Islands', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caribbean Area', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harrison, Jim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-30T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af728784-c8f8-5027-9457-ab5682433105', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af728784-c8f8-5027-9457-ab5682433105'}, {'abstract': 'Young adults who decide to abandon their cloistered Jewish communities have only one another — and a single organization — to help them navigate the alternate reality of modern-day New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/magazine/the-high-price-of-leaving-ultra-orthodox-life.html', 'snippet': 'Young adults who decide to abandon their cloistered Jewish communities have only one another — and a single organization — to help them navigate the alternate reality of modern-day New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursdays, the nonprofit organization Footsteps hosts a drop-in group for its membership of formerly ultra-Orthodox Jews, who mostly refer to themselves as “off the derech.” “Derech” means “path” in Hebrew, and “off the derech,” or O.T.D. for short, is how their ultra-Orthodox families and friends refer to them when they break away from these tight-knit, impermeable communities, as in: “Did you hear that Shaindel’s daughter Rivkie is off the derech? I heard she has a smartphone and has been going to museums.” So even though the term is burdened with the yoke of the very thing they are trying to flee, members remain huddled together under “O.T.D.” on their blogs and in their Facebook groups, where their favored hashtag is #itgetsbesser — besser meaning “better” in Yiddish. Sometimes someone will pop up on a message board or in an email group and say, “Shouldn’t we decide to call ourselves something else?” But it never takes. Reclamations are messy.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02footsteps1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02footsteps1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02footsteps1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02mag-02footsteps-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02mag-02footsteps-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02mag-02footsteps-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02footsteps1/02mag-02footsteps-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The High Price of Leaving Ultra-Orthodox Life', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apostates Anonymous', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hasidism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yiddish Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Footsteps (Nonprofit)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-30T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taffy Brodesser-Akner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taffy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brodesser-Akner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c43ccf74-1816-5799-a4b4-22cefc5f2f0d', 'word_count': 4762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c43ccf74-1816-5799-a4b4-22cefc5f2f0d'}, {'abstract': 'What are the benefits to a college or university that highly specializes?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/learning/what-specialty-college-would-you-create.html', 'snippet': 'What are the benefits to a college or university that highly specializes?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In your ideal world, what type of college or university would you attend?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/19/nyregion/19MARITIME1/19MARITIME1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Specialty College Would You Create?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-03-30T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Crosson Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': 'Crosson', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec47132a-d7a0-56ff-8f01-85e8d73eaa15', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec47132a-d7a0-56ff-8f01-85e8d73eaa15'}, {'abstract': 'A youthful love of its articles and photographs led the writer to a life of travel — and to an unexpected encounter with a fellow adventurer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-life-magazine.html', 'snippet': 'A youthful love of its articles and photographs led the writer to a life of travel — and to an unexpected encounter with a fellow adventurer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Life magazine was like life itself for me for about 12 years of my early youth, until we stopped renewing our subscription. I remember the images of the Korean War, the movie “Cleopatra,” the funeral of William Faulkner (reported by William Styron), the space program and the Vietnam War. Life’s photographs were brave and brilliant, capturing the human moment. Reading Life in a bungalow in Medford, Mass., I beheld a world that was remote and alluring, places I would never see, parties I would never attend, millionaires and up-and-comers I would never know. It was how the mass of people lived, vicariously, following the fortunes of famous names in magazines; how they still live, overlooked, grinding away, not celebrated, not up-and-coming.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1125, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/magazine/02lor/02lor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: Life Magazine', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life Magazine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Life (Magazine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hawaii', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKay, Gardner', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-03-30T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Theroux', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Theroux', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8ef39f2-f9b5-58ee-bd28-199e6b447803', 'word_count': 955, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8ef39f2-f9b5-58ee-bd28-199e6b447803'}, {'abstract': 'Travel to a nontouristy neighborhood, join a supper club, learn cooking skills or take a tour with residents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/travel/how-to-feel-at-home-on-the-road.html', 'snippet': 'Travel to a nontouristy neighborhood, join a supper club, learn cooking skills or take a tour with residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travel is, by definition, dislocating, a condition that the trend toward experiential travel and the connectivity provided by the internet aim to ease.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Tim