 '_id': 'nyt://article/148e11a8-5c6c-5d20-8288-5213885368f2', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/148e11a8-5c6c-5d20-8288-5213885368f2'}, {'abstract': 'A new series from the people behind “This American Life” surpasses their earlier hit in a number of ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/30/arts/true-crime-podcast-s-town-serial.html', 'snippet': 'A new series from the people behind “This American Life” surpasses their earlier hit in a number of ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2014, the true-crime podcast “Serial” began like this: A tipster asked a radio journalist to investigate a murder. “S-Town,” the new narrative podcast from the creators of “Serial,” kicks off in a similar fashion. This time, the tipster is John B. McLemore, an eccentric antique clock restorer from Woodstock, Ala. The journalist is Brian Reed, a longtime producer for “This American Life.” When Mr. McLemore tells Mr. Reed that a Woodstock rich boy got away with murder and has been bragging about it ever since, Mr. Reed agrees to head down from New York to check it out. But it soon becomes clear that the homicide story is a bit of a red herring. It’s the hook to get Mr. Reed on the line.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/31/arts/01NEWSERIAL/01NEWSERIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/31/arts/01NEWSERIAL/01NEWSERIAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/31/arts/01NEWSERIAL/01NEWSERIAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/31/a