kReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kinsley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kinsley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b66ea9eb-0a9d-5157-b0c3-20427b52cfc8', 'word_count': 658, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b66ea9eb-0a9d-5157-b0c3-20427b52cfc8'}, {'abstract': 'Two women, both injured in the Boston Marathon bombing, find self-understanding and love, while a third takes up running to reshape her body and her life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/31/books/review/in-the-long-run.html', 'snippet': 'Two women, both injured in the Boston Marathon bombing, find self-understanding and love, while a third takes up running to reshape her body and her life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What makes a story worthy of an entire book? This question arises frequently when I work with students who are writing memoirs, often after they’ve produced 20 pages of riveting prose about the most dramatic thing that ever happened to them only to find they’re out of things to say. When writing about yourself, it’s difficult enough to ease up on the pedal of self-flattery in the interests of truth. But to confront your story’s limitations head-on and admit it’s better suited to 20 pages than 220 pages can be a painful reckoning — though, as I tell my students, not nearly as painful as trying to spread a ho-hum back story into a thin layer across a whole book. As Stephen King said in his exemplary literary craft guide, “On Writing,” “The most important things to remember about back story are that (a) everyone has a history and (b) most of it isn’t very interesting.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 207, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 207}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/04/02/books/review/0402-BKS-Egos/0402-BKS-Egos-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Long Run', 'kicker': 'Egos', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Memoir', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Perfect Strangers: Friendship, Strength, and Recovery After Boston's Worst Day (Book)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sdoia, Roseann', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Taking My Life Back: My Stor